{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-167650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/assets/22a909d34b63517bf5ccf90560a8070d20e1aa91a6c9eaf8f4e5326208b9d751.png)\n\n \n\nThe shell above is obtained by rotating the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [0,1]$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis, as shown above. For each point $(x,y,z)$ on the shell, the charge density (electric charge per unit surface area) of the shell is given by the function \n\n$$\nd(x,y,z)=\\frac{x({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the curvature $κ(x)$ of the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at $x=0.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $κ(0)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=4x,\\,{y}^{″}(x)=4\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|4|}{{[1+{(4x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{(1+16{x}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{4}{{(1+16(0)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{1}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e4d05e83d6b79dd65aad337f01ab871cf0d2786e7721f8fa052300b571c72244"} {"question" "q-167650" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167650" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/assets/22a909d34b63517bf5ccf90560a8070d20e1aa91a6c9eaf8f4e5326208b9d751.png)\n\n \n\nThe shell above is obtained by rotating the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [0,1]$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis, as shown above. For each point $(x,y,z)$ on the shell, the charge density (electric charge per unit surface area) of the shell is given by the function \n\n$$\nd(x,y,z)=\\frac{x({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the curvature $κ(x)$ of the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at $x=0.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $κ(0)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWhat is the radius of curvature $R(x)$ of the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at the point $x=0?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $R(0)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In part 1, we found that the curvature of the curve at the point $x=0$ is\n\n$$\nκ(0)=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(0)=\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e4d05e83d6b79dd65aad337f01ab871cf0d2786e7721f8fa052300b571c72244"} {"question" "q-167652" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167652" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/assets/22a909d34b63517bf5ccf90560a8070d20e1aa91a6c9eaf8f4e5326208b9d751.png)\n\n \n\nThe shell above is obtained by rotating the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [0,1]$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis, as shown above. For each point $(x,y,z)$ on the shell, the charge density (electric charge per unit surface area) of the shell is given by the function \n\n$$\nd(x,y,z)=\\frac{x({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the curvature $κ(x)$ of the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at $x=0.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $κ(0)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nWhat is the radius of curvature $R(x)$ of the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at the point $x=0?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $R(0)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{4}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWrite down the integral that gives the surface area of the shell.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Surface area $\\,=4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2})=4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+(4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e4d05e83d6b79dd65aad337f01ab871cf0d2786e7721f8fa052300b571c72244"} {"question" "q-167651" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167651" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x^2\\sqrt{1+16x^2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/assets/22a909d34b63517bf5ccf90560a8070d20e1aa91a6c9eaf8f4e5326208b9d751.png)\n\n \n\nThe shell above is obtained by rotating the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [0,1]$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis, as shown above. For each point $(x,y,z)$ on the shell, the charge density (electric charge per unit surface area) of the shell is given by the function \n\n$$\nd(x,y,z)=\\frac{x({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the curvature $κ(x)$ of the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at $x=0.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $κ(0)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nWhat is the radius of curvature $R(x)$ of the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at the point $x=0?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $R(0)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{4}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWrite down the integral that gives the surface area of the shell.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Surface area $\\,=4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $x^2\\sqrt{1+16x^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}x.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWrite down the missing parts of a parametrization of our shell below.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $r(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩,\\,x\\in [0,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/assets/56a380b075d10862170ed9c9a45c07dddff3eaef1ea9fb3109a61cc3d27b5ac4.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(x)=2{x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,1].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [0,\\,1],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e4d05e83d6b79dd65aad337f01ab871cf0d2786e7721f8fa052300b571c72244"} {"question" "q-167647" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167647" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x^2\\sin(\\theta)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167647" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167647" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/assets/22a909d34b63517bf5ccf90560a8070d20e1aa91a6c9eaf8f4e5326208b9d751.png)\n\n \n\nThe shell above is obtained by rotating the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [0,1]$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis, as shown above. For each point $(x,y,z)$ on the shell, the charge density (electric charge per unit surface area) of the shell is given by the function \n\n$$\nd(x,y,z)=\\frac{x({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the curvature $κ(x)$ of the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at $x=0.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $κ(0)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nWhat is the radius of curvature $R(x)$ of the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at the point $x=0?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $R(0)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{4}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWrite down the integral that gives the surface area of the shell.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Surface area $\\,=4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $x^2\\sqrt{1+16x^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}x.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nWrite down the missing parts of a parametrization of our shell below.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $r(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x^2\\sin(\\theta)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩,\\,x\\in [0,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the norm of the fundamental vector product for the surface $r(x,\\theta ),$ found in the previous step.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nIn part 4, we got the surface\n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [0,1],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂\\theta }(x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂\\theta }(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂\\theta }(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8{x}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +8{x}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8{x}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ),\\,-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8{x}^{3},\\,-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding norm is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(8{x}^{3})^{2}+(-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64{x}^{6}+4{x}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{x}^{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64{x}^{6}+4{x}^{4}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64{x}^{6}+4{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e4d05e83d6b79dd65aad337f01ab871cf0d2786e7721f8fa052300b571c72244"} {"question" "q-167648" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167648" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x^2\\sqrt{1+16x^2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/assets/22a909d34b63517bf5ccf90560a8070d20e1aa91a6c9eaf8f4e5326208b9d751.png)\n\n \n\nThe shell above is obtained by rotating the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [0,1]$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis, as shown above. For each point $(x,y,z)$ on the shell, the charge density (electric charge per unit surface area) of the shell is given by the function \n\n$$\nd(x,y,z)=\\frac{x({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the curvature $κ(x)$ of the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at $x=0.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $κ(0)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nWhat is the radius of curvature $R(x)$ of the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at the point $x=0?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $R(0)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{4}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWrite down the integral that gives the surface area of the shell.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Surface area $\\,=4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $x^2\\sqrt{1+16x^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}x.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nWrite down the missing parts of a parametrization of our shell below.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $r(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x^2\\sin(\\theta)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩,\\,x\\in [0,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFind the norm of the fundamental vector product for the surface $r(x,\\theta ),$ found in the previous step.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x^2\\sqrt{1+16x^2}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nIf we denote our surface of revolution by $S,$ then the total electric charge of the shell is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $Q={∬}_{S}^{}d(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nPreviously, we determined that our surface can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [0,1],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nand the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖=2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(x,\\theta )$ into $d(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(r(x,\\theta )) & =\\frac{x({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x((2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}d(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}d(r(x,\\theta ))‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}}\\cdot 2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,8{x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}8{x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[{x}^{8}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e4d05e83d6b79dd65aad337f01ab871cf0d2786e7721f8fa052300b571c72244"} {"question" "q-167649" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167649" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167658" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/assets/22a909d34b63517bf5ccf90560a8070d20e1aa91a6c9eaf8f4e5326208b9d751.png)\n\n \n\nThe shell above is obtained by rotating the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [0,1]$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis, as shown above. For each point $(x,y,z)$ on the shell, the charge density (electric charge per unit surface area) of the shell is given by the function \n\n$$\nd(x,y,z)=\\frac{x({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the curvature $κ(x)$ of the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at $x=0.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $κ(0)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nWhat is the radius of curvature $R(x)$ of the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at the point $x=0?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $R(0)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{4}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWrite down the integral that gives the surface area of the shell.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Surface area $\\,=4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $x^2\\sqrt{1+16x^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}x.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nWrite down the missing parts of a parametrization of our shell below.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $r(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x^2\\sin(\\theta)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩,\\,x\\in [0,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFind the norm of the fundamental vector product for the surface $r(x,\\theta ),$ found in the previous step.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x^2\\sqrt{1+16x^2}$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nIf we denote our surface of revolution by $S,$ then the total electric charge of the shell is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $Q={∬}_{S}^{}d(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2\\pi$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFor the transformation $r:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined as\n\n$$\nr(x)=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ \\theta \\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${r}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & a \\\\ b & -2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a+b+c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167658" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{r}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{r}_{1}}{∂\\theta } \\\\ \\frac{∂{r}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{r}_{2}}{∂\\theta } \\\\ \\frac{∂{r}_{3}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{r}_{3}}{∂\\theta }\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {r}_{x}^{′} & {r}_{\\theta }^{′} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe computed the partial derivatives in part 5:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}]\\,{r}_{\\theta }^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂\\theta }(x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂\\theta }(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂\\theta }(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b+c & =0+4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2}+4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167658" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e4d05e83d6b79dd65aad337f01ab871cf0d2786e7721f8fa052300b571c72244"} {"question" "q-167658" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"} {"question" "q-167658" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4x\\cos(\\theta)+2x^2\\cos(\\theta)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083460/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ca58acd25dfa9943638fe147242c1ec645eedeceeb2f112f27baa1aa3b0fecc"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2871 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
